INT. ABANDONED SUBWAY STATION, PLATFORM - NIGHT

A ghost station, tiles cracked, an old clock stopped at 4:17. A single working fluorescent flickers over a table set with four chairs and a briefcase. Dust motes in the light. The tunnel mouths gape on either side.

ARVID SOLHEIM-MBEKI (50s, gray overcoat, patient) sits with his hands folded on the briefcase. Opposite him sits IRENE DUCASSE-ONYANGO (40s, tailored coat, burner phone face-down by her elbow). At the table's end sits LORCAN FITZGERALD-PARK (30s, hoodie, jaw tight). And standing in a tunnel mouth, half in shadow, arms folded, is TAMSIN VELÁZQUEZ-ROY (30s, leather jacket, a stillness that makes the others look jumpy).

ARVID
Thank you for coming.

IRENE
You gave us little choice.

ARVID
I gave you a table. A chair. A briefcase.

IRENE
A briefcase we haven't opened.

ARVID
Quite.

LORCAN
Let's just open it.

ARVID
Not yet.

LORCAN
Why not yet?

ARVID
Because once it's open, we are four people who have seen what is inside it. And we cannot be less than that again.

Irene turns her phone face-up. Checks the screen. Turns it face-down.

IRENE
Where is Tamsin sitting?

ARVID
She prefers standing.

IRENE
She's making me nervous.

TAMSIN
(from the tunnel)
Good.

LORCAN
Can we please just--

ARVID
Lorcan. Do you remember the night of the fourteenth?

LORCAN
No.

ARVID
Think.

LORCAN
I was at home.

ARVID
Alone?

LORCAN
Yes.

ARVID
Irene, do you remember it?

IRENE
Vaguely.

ARVID
Where were you?

IRENE
At the gallery.

ARVID
Until what hour?

IRENE
Late.

ARVID
How late?

IRENE
I don't see why this matters.

ARVID
It matters because on the night of the fourteenth, a man named Dominic Ashworth was found at the bottom of a staircase. And none of us has ever said a word about it.

Silence. The fluorescent buzzes. The stopped clock watches.

LORCAN
I didn't touch him.

ARVID
No one said you did.

LORCAN
I wasn't even there.

ARVID
No one said you were.

IRENE
(too evenly)
Arvid, what is in the briefcase?

ARVID
A reason to be honest.

Tamsin steps from the tunnel into the light. She walks to the table and puts a hand flat on the briefcase lid, next to Arvid's.

TAMSIN
It's his. Dominic's.

LORCAN
His what?

TAMSIN
His record. Every payment. Every name.

IRENE
Who told you that?

TAMSIN
He did. Before.

Irene's hand goes still on the phone. Lorcan leans forward.

LORCAN
That's why you called us.

ARVID
That's why I called you.

IRENE
To threaten us.

ARVID
To give you a choice. Open it, burn it, or walk away with it unopened and live as people who might someday regret that.

LORCAN
That's three options.

ARVID
It's more than he got.

Nobody speaks. Irene reaches out, slowly, and rests her fingertips on the lock. Lorcan stands, chair scraping, and takes two steps back toward the stairs.

TAMSIN
(quietly)
Lorcan.

He stops.

TAMSIN (CONT'D)
Sit down.

He sits. Irene presses the latch. It clicks.

The lid lifts one inch. A light from inside spills across her face.

ARVID
(softly)
There you are.
